WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.454
Security-relevant API logs should help
teams understand who called what,

2
00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:10.410
from where, against which resource, with
what result, and at what volume.

3
00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.130
Useful fields often include timestamp,
request ID, caller identity,

4
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:19.850
client application, tenant, endpoint,
operation, status, latency,

5
00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.604
decision category, and safe error code.

6
00:00:22.684 --> 00:00:28.346
Logs should support investigation and
monitoring without turning into a second copy

7
00:00:28.426 --> 00:00:29.999
of sensitive payloads.

8
00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:33.146
Avoid secrets and sensitive payloads in
logs.

9
00:00:33.226 --> 00:00:37.944
Authorization headers, bearer tokens, API
keys, cookies, passwords,

10
00:00:38.024 --> 00:00:39.990
payment data, personal data,

11
00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.396
and full request or response bodies should
be handled carefully.

12
00:00:44.476 --> 00:00:48.879
Redaction should be designed, tested, and
applied consistently.

13
00:00:48.959 --> 00:00:54.386
Audit trails should capture meaningful
actions such as administrative changes,

14
00:00:54.466 --> 00:00:58.712
access to sensitive records, failed
authorization decisions,

15
00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.916
and changes to API credentials.

16
00:01:00.996 --> 00:01:04.613
Testing should cover the contract and the
abuse cases.

17
00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:10.119
API contract tests confirm that required
fields, response shapes, status codes,

18
00:01:10.199 --> 00:01:12.873
and schema expectations remain stable.

19
00:01:12.953 --> 00:01:17.357
Negative tests send malformed, missing,
oversized, unexpected,

20
00:01:17.437 --> 00:01:19.167
and type-confused input.

21
00:01:19.247 --> 00:01:24.359
Authorization tests verify that callers
can only perform allowed actions on

22
00:01:24.439 --> 00:01:27.113
resources they are permitted to access.

23
00:01:27.193 --> 00:01:32.619
Fuzzing at a high level can help exercise
parsers and edge cases that hand-written

24
00:01:32.699 --> 00:01:33.486
tests miss.

25
00:01:33.566 --> 00:01:38.677
Reviewing OpenAPI specifications and
production behavior closes the loop.

26
00:01:38.757 --> 00:01:44.026
Specifications can reveal undocumented
endpoints, missing authentication,

27
00:01:44.106 --> 00:01:47.252
broad parameters, inconsistent error
models,

28
00:01:47.332 --> 00:01:51.893
or response fields that do not match
least-privilege expectations.

29
00:01:51.973 --> 00:01:56.692
Production monitoring can reveal unusual
volume, repeated failures,

30
00:01:56.772 --> 00:02:02.355
enumeration patterns, expensive queries,
and clients using deprecated behavior.

31
00:02:02.435 --> 00:02:07.940
Observability is part of API security
because unknown behavior cannot be managed.
